<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-topological-groups" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Topological groups</title>
  <p>
    In this section we define and give some examples of topological groups.
  </p>
  <definition xml:id="def-topological-group">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>G</m> be a group and a topological space. We say that <m>G</m> is a <em>topological group</em> if the group operation <m>G\times G\to G</m>, given by <m>(g,h)\mapsto gh</m> and the inverse map <m>G\to G</m> given by <m>g\mapsto g^{-1}</m> are continous.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
      <example xml:id="GL-as-topological-group"><title>(Group of invertible matrices as a topological space)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          We consider <m>\GL_n(\R)=\{A\in M_n(\R):\det A\neq 0\}</m> as a subset of <m>\R^{n^2}</m>, and we give subspace topology on <m>\GL_n(\R)</m>. As the entries of product of matrices <m>A,B\in \GL_n(\R)</m> depend continuously on entries of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, the matrix multiplication map <m>\GL_n(\R)\times \GL_n(\R)\to \GL_n(\R)</m> is continuous. Furthermore, the entries of the inverse of <m>A</m> depends continuously on the entries of <m>A</m>. Hence, the inverse map <m>\GL_n(\R)\to \GL_n(\R)</m> given by <m>A\mapsto A^{-1}</m> is continuous.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="example-SL-SO">
    <title>(Special linear and special orthogonal groups)</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Similar to <xref ref="GL-as-topological-group"/>, we get that the following subgroups of <m>\GL_n(\R)</m> are topological groups. <me>\SL_n(\R)=\left\{A\in M_n(\R):\det A=1\right\}\quad\text{and}\quad\SO_n(\R)=\left\{A\in \SL_n(\R):AA^t=I_n\right\}</me>
        </p>
    </statement>
    </example>

    <exercise>
    <title>(Equivalent condition)</title>
    
    
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that the two conditions in <xref ref="def-topological-group"/> is equivalent to the following.
          <me>
            \text{The mapping }(g,h)\mapsto gh^{-1} \text{ from }G\times G\to G \text{ is continuous}.
          </me>
          
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
    <title>(Translations)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>G</m> be a topological group. Fix <m>g_0\in G</m>. Show that mapping <m>\ell_{g_0}\colon G\to G</m> given by <m>g\mapsto g_0g</m> and <m>r_{g_0}\colon G\to G</m> given by <m>g\mapsto gg_0</m> are homeomorphisms.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="addtive-group-euclidean-space">
    <title>(Additive group of the Euclidean space)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that <m>+\colon\R^n\times\R^n\to\R^n</m> given by <me>\left((x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_n),(y_1,y_2,\ldots,y_n)\right)\mapsto\left(x_1+y_1,x_2+y_2,\ldots,x_n+y_n\right)</me> is continuous.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

</section>